\section{Introduction}
Virgin Islands English Creole (ISO 639-3 vic) is an English-based creole of the U.S. and British Virgin Islands. This report rests on a single web search, which turned up almost nothing school-oriented. The extinct Dutch-based Negerhollands is a different language and is listed only to keep the two apart.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No textbooks, primers or school programmes for Virgin Islands English Creole were found.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Overview article on Virgin Islands Creole (Wikipedia): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virgin_Islands_Creole}
\item Master's thesis on Virgin Islands English lexifier Creole and politeness practices on St.\ Croix, which touches on language policy in education (University of Puerto Rico repository): \url{https://repositorio.upr.edu/handle/11721/2924}
\item Slides on the languages of the Virgin Islands: \url{https://www.yellowpigs.net/virginislands/language/vislides.pdf}
\item Page on the Virgin Islands language (edu-cyberpg.com): \url{https://edu-cyberpg.com/Linguistics/vi.html}
\end{itemize}
For the separate, extinct Negerhollands (not vic): the Living Dictionaries site, \url{https://livingdictionaries.app/negerhollands/about}, and the Stein and van der Voort edition of Oldendorp's dictionary, \url{https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/9783110937985/html}.

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found for Virgin Islands English Creole itself.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Locals often call the language a ``dialect'', and it is not known to be taught in schools. A curriculum would need new documentation work, probably starting from the St.\ Croix thesis and its bibliography.
\end{document}
